{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "mistralai/mistral-small-4-119b-2603", "content": "**Raymond III Count of Tripoli**\n\nRaymond III was Count of Tripoli from 1152 to 1187. He was the son of Raymond II of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel. Raymond III became count at a young age after his father was assassinated in 1152. His mother Hodierna ruled as regent until he came of age. Raymond III was involved in the politics of the Crusader states from an early age. He participated in the Battle of Harim in 1164 where he was captured by Nur ad-Din and held prisoner until 1172. Upon his release he resumed his role in Tripoli and later became regent of the Kingdom of Jerusalem for Baldwin IV from 1174 to 1177 and again from 1184 to 1185. Raymond III played a significant role in the internal struggles of the Crusader states often mediating between different factions.\n\n*highlighted section part 1*\n**Regency and Politics**\nRaymond IIIs regency over Baldwin IV began in 1174 after the death of King Amalric. Baldwin IV was a leper and Raymond acted as his regent. During this period Raymond III worked to maintain stability in the kingdom. He faced opposition from factions led by Guy of Lusignan and others who sought greater power. Raymond III negotiated truces with Saladin and managed to keep the kingdom from collapsing despite internal divisions. His regency ended in 1177 when Baldwin IV came of age though Raymond continued to wield influence.\n\n*highlighted section part 2*\n**Conflict with Guy of Lusignan**\nOne of the major challenges Raymond III faced was the rise of Guy of Lusignan. Guy married Sibylla of Jerusalem in 1180 and became a leading figure in the kingdom. Raymond III opposed Guys ambitions and clashed with him over control of the kingdom. In 1183 Raymond III supported the coronation of Baldwin V as co-king but Guy and Sibylla sought to undermine this. The conflict between Raymond III and Guy of Lusignan weakened the Crusader states at a critical time.\n\n*highlighted section part 3*\n**Later Years and Legacy**\nRaymond III remained an important figure until his death in 1187. He continued to play a role in the politics of the Crusader states even after his regency ended. In 1187 he was involved in the lead-up to the Battle of Hattin which resulted in a catastrophic defeat for the Crusaders. Raymond III died shortly after the battle and his death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli and the Kingdom of Jerusalem. His legacy includes efforts to maintain stability and his role in the complex politics of the Crusader states during a turbulent period.", "logprobs": [{"token": "**", "logprob": -0.16611935198307037}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.40981432795524597}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.00013052565918769687}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.030098311603069305}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.00010382589971413836}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -6.9141146923357155e-06}, {"token": "**\n\n", "logprob": -0.7171970009803772}, {"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.050112128257751465}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.9311717551317997e-05}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0006785474251955748}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.09650948643684387}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -0.25321149826049805}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -1.1920922133867862e-06}, {"token": " Tripoli", "logprob": -6.556489552167477e-06}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.0011835244949907064}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0009390473715029657}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.5020257706055418e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -0.00015209948469419032}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -0.0014892450999468565}, {"token": "2", "logprob": -0.00011419598013162613}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -0.47422391176223755}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.008648795075714588}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -1.9073468138230965e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -3.4927710657939315e-05}, {"token"